<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodora Porphyrogenita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–<a href="/years/1056">1056</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodora Porphyrogenita was Byzantine empress from 1042 until her death in 1056, and sole ruler from 1055, the last sovereign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>. The youngest daughter of Constantine VIII, she refused to marry Romanos Argyros and, accused of plotting against her sister Zoë, was confined in the monastery of Petrion in 1031. A popular uprising against Michael V in 1042 brought her out to reign jointly with Zoë, until Zoë's marriage to Constantine IX passed power to him. On his death in 1055 she took the throne again and presided in the Senate herself; she died refusing to marry or name an heir, ending the dynasty's 189-year rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodora-porphyrogenita.jpg" alt="Theodora Porphyrogenita" width="330" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dumbarton Oaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_seal_of_Empress_Theodora.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodora was the third and youngest daughter of Emperor Constantine VIII and Helena, daughter of Alypius. Porphyrogenita, born into the purple, was the title of a child born in the capital to a reigning emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> prevented his nieces from marrying into the Byzantine nobility, since a husband would gain a claim on the throne, and Theodora lived in obscurity in the women's quarters of the palace.</p><p>She refused to marry Romanos Argyros, whom her father had chosen as successor, saying that he was already married and that as third cousins they were too closely related. Zoë married him instead. Accused in 1031 of plotting with Constantine Diogenes, the Archon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, she was confined in the monastery of Petrion and forced to take the monastic habit, and she stayed there for eleven years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Michael V, adopted by Zoë, banished her to the Princes' Islands. A popular uprising began on 19 April 1042, in support of both sisters. Theodora at first refused to leave Petrion, but both sisters were crowned on 21 April and Michael V escaped to a monastery. Theodora ordered him blinded and made a monk. She was officially the junior empress but in practice the driving force of the joint administration, which sought to curb the sale of public offices and to improve the administration of justice.</p><p>After two months of acrimony Zoë married Constantine IX on 11 June 1042, and power passed to him. After Zoë's death in 1050 Theodora seems to have retired to a convent. When Constantine IX lay dying in January 1055, his councillors favoured Nikephoros Proteuon, but her supporters persuaded Theodora to assert her right. She was brought to the Great Palace and the imperial guard proclaimed her emperor. She purged senior officials, controlled the nobles and presided in person in the Senate, and her coins and seals called her Theodora, Augusta and porphyrogenita.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Contemporary judgements varied. Michael Psellus called the joint reign a failure, while John Scylitzes held that the sisters conscientiously rectified the abuses of earlier reigns. Of her sole rule it is said that she damaged her reputation by excessive severity toward private enemies and by relying on menials as advisors.</p><p>At seventy-six she refused all suggestions of marriage and would not name an heir. She fell gravely ill in late August 1056, and on 31 August her advisers chose Michael Bringas as successor, taking a nod from the speechless empress as assent. He was crowned as Michael VI, and Theodora died a few hours later, ending the 189-year rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodora Porphyrogenita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Theodora Porphyrogenita</b>, Byzantine empress since 1042 and sole ruler since 1055, died childless after a sudden gastric illness.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodora Porphyrogenita</b>, who had reigned as empress since 1042, became sole ruler of the Byzantine Empire until her death in August 1056.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1029" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Theodora Porphyrogenita"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1029">What was here in 1029? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
